\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Remark}
\title{Status of the potential construction}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{research-programme}

\p{The costed potential is a proposed question format. It is grounded in
standard expected utility, held-out evaluation, and constrained optimization.
Its particular protocol class, trust predicate, and lifecycle cost vector are
FTIP choices.}

\p{The diagram shows the order of dependence. Training produces a policy;
independent evaluation measures it; accounting decides whether the protocol
belongs to the feasible set.}

\tikzfig{\begin{tikzpicture}[
  box/.style={draw, rounded corners=2pt, align=center, font=\small,
    text width=35mm, minimum height=10mm},
  flow/.style={-{Stealth[length=2mm]}, thick}
]
\node[box] (base) at (0,1.4) {base artifact $M_0$};
\node[box] (train) at (5.2,1.4) {trusted protocol $P$};
\node[box] (eval) at (5.2,-1.0) {independent performance $J$};
\node[box] (cost) at (0,-1.0) {lifecycle cost $C(P)$};
\draw[flow] (base) -- (train);
\draw[flow] (train) -- (eval);
\draw[flow] (train) -- (cost);
\draw[flow] (cost) -- node[font=\scriptsize, fill=white, inner sep=1pt]
  {feasibility} (eval);
\end{tikzpicture}}

\p{The first mathematical task is to study finite special cases. A useful
result should expose the assumptions under which feedback quality, support,
update stability, or compute allocation changes the attainable set.}
